<h1>Brian Carlisle</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://www.waldgrave.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.waldgrave.com/chigui" rel="nofollow noopener">Brian Carlisle&#39;s policies are published on his nonprofit&#39;s website, waldgrave.com. Tap here to read them there, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>Housing: Change zoning and permitting so more homes get built, protect two-flats, and bring back single-room housing for people on the street.</p>
<p>Safety: Put money into detectives so shootings get solved, expand jobs-plus-therapy programs, and send mental health teams to crisis calls around the clock.</p>
<p>Money: Tie pension reform to a new income tax, run more city work in-house instead of through contractors, and replace the property tax with a tax on land value.</p>
<p>Also: Replace every lead pipe, run trains and buses more often, and adopt ranked-choice voting. Most policies name a city that already did it.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>Only Carlisle: A land value tax, a new income tax tied to pension reform, ranked-choice voting, and ending aldermen&#39;s control over zoning.</p>
<p>Mental health: Holberg, Quigley, and Carlisle would send mental health teams to crisis calls. Quigley and Carlisle would also reopen neighborhood mental health clinics.</p>
<p>Cash to households: Carlisle backs guaranteed income, Cardenas proposes $3,000 yearly stipends for working families, and Brooks proposes a $3,500 credit for family caregivers.</p>
<p>Not covered: Recruiting businesses and jobs, which Holberg, Mendoza, Brewer, Kelly, and Su put near the top.</p>
